\chapter{Memory Architecture: Sublinear Metadata, Granularity, and Design Goals}
\label{ch:memory}

Building upon the initial exploratory prototype developed in the
Telix\index{Telix} repository\footcite{telix_proto}, this proposal
outlines a comprehensive redesign of the Virtual Memory (VM) and
physical memory management subsystems. Rather than optimizing for a
single metric, the proposed architecture reconciles a broad, disparate
set of memory management design goals that frequently contend with one
another in traditional operating system kernels.

\section{System Design Goals for Memory Management}
\label{sec:mem_goals}

The proposed Telix\index{Telix} memory subsystem is guided by a
multi-faceted design matrix intended to solve long-standing bottlenecks
and issues in systems software:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Sublinear Management Metadata Footprint:} Achieving
	    true sublinear scaling for internal bookkeeping structures
	    by employing \emph{pervasive extent-based internal
	    accounting} across both physical and virtual layers,
	    eliminating boot-time-reserved static descriptor arrays
	    (such as traditional Linux\index{Linux} \texttt{mem\_map}
	    \index{mem_map@\texttt{mem\_map}}).

    \item \textbf{TLB\index{TLB} Reach and Superpaging
	    \index{superpaging} Performance:} Maximizing execution speed
	    and minimizing hardware Translation Lookaside Buffer
	    (TLB\index{TLB}) misses through opportunistic superpaging,
	    leveraging modern hardware {page size spectra}
	    \index{page size spectrum}.

    \item \textbf{Cluster-Readiness\index{cluster} and Distributed
	    Support:} Accommodating process migration and distributed
	    node topologies without requiring a full NO Remote Memory
	    Access (NORMA\index{NORMA}) architecture. As Miloji\v{c}i\'c
	    et al. demonstrated, full NORMA\index{NORMA} incurs
	    prohibitive latency, leading to its general abandonment.
	    Instead, the VM subsystem focuses on targeted cluster-wide
	    support, such as cross-node page mapping invalidations and
	    TLB\index{TLB} shootdowns in response to file truncation or
	    distributed replicated page unmapping.

    \item \textbf{Adjusting Fragmentation\index{fragmentation}
	    Frontiers:} Utilizing ABI-preserving
	    \pageclustering\ to tune the frontier between
	    internal\index{fragmentation!internal} and
	    \externalfragmentation, specifically guaranteeing that
	    smaller-size \superpage\ allocations never
	    fail due to {external memory fragmentation}
	    \index{fragmentation!external}.

    \item \textbf{Low-Latency Fault Handling and Tail Packing:} Reducing
	    fault latency through incremental faulting strategies for
	    Copy-On-Write (COW) and Zero-Fill-On-Demand (ZFOD) anonymous
	    memory, alongside in-memory tail packing in the page cache
	    for small files to optimize bitblitting and small I/O
	    operations.

    \item \textbf{Hardware Capability Integration:} Mapping
	    software-level memory range tokens directly onto
	    hardware-enforced capability architectures (such as CHERI
	    and ARM Morello) to enforce spatial and temporal memory
	    safety at hardware speeds.
\end{enumerate}

\section{ABI-Preserving Page Clustering and Fragmentation Frontiers}
\label{sec:mem_clustering}

{Page clustering}\index{page clustering} is frequently misunderstood as
a mechanism for reducing virtual metadata or achieving sublinear
scaling, and is sometimes incorrectly assumed to be inextricably linked
to superpaging\index{superpaging}. In reality,
\pageclustering\ achieves only a linear reduction in physical
metadata in traditional kernel designs, while leaving virtual
{page table}\index{page table} footprints completely unaltered. In
Telix's\index{Telix} design, the {page descriptor}
\index{page descriptor} memory footprint reduction effect is
particularly inconsequential because the architecture eschews global
descriptor arrays entirely. Rather,
\pageclustering\ is intended to serve a different, highly
strategic purpose: \textit{tuning the frontier between internal
\index{fragmentation!internal} and {external fragmentation}
\index{fragmentation!external}}.

\subsection{Nomenclature and Structural Abstraction}

To maintain rigorous compatibility with historical literature and
codebases, this document adopts a unified nomenclature mapping across
hardware granularities and historical implementations:

