Modern \pageclustering
\footcite{chambers03clustering, dickins01clustering,
arcangeli07clustering, shutsemau26clustering} strictly preserves
application binary interfaces (ABIs\index{ABI}) by keeping $K$ as the
fundamental mapping granularity. For analytical purposes, we adopt the
Greek equivalents ($\kappa$, $\zeta$, $\beta$) to denote the base-2
logarithms ($\lg$) of these respective quantities.

When Telix allocates memory, physical allocation occurs in units of $Z$.
However, virtual memory operations continue to address individual
$K$-sized fragments. For example, an \texttt{mmap()} targeting a
sub-range within an allocation unit sets only the specific $K$-sized
Page Table Entries (PTEs) corresponding to those fragments. Mapping $N$
fragments of size $K$ within a $Z$ allocation is handled via optimized
vector loops over the page table.

\subsection{Qualitative Guarantees and Sparse Spectra ($W$)}

Let $W$ (\emph{Sprungweite}) denote the jump order (the logarithm of the
factor), or mathematical distance, between successive superpage
\index{superpaging} sizes in a given architecture's hardware {page size
spectrum}\index{page size spectrum}.

Page sizes $g$ (for \foreignlanguage{german}{Seitengröße}) generally
follow the pattern:
\begin{equation}
    g_n = K \cdot 2^{W \cdot n}
\end{equation}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Cross-Reference of Page Clustered Superpaging Terms and Codebase Macro Equivalents}
\label{tab:superpaging_terms}
\begin{tabularx}{0.9\textwidth}{l X}
\toprule
\textbf{Concept} & \textbf{Symbol} \\
\midrule
$n$-th Superpage Size & $g_n$ (\emph{Seitengröße}) \\
Superpage Order Increment & $W$ (\emph{Sprungweite}) \\
Superpage Increase Factor & $F$ (\emph{Sprungfaktor}) \\
First Non-Trivial Superpage Order & $\Omega$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

{Page clustering}\index{page clustering} provides two distinct
operational benefits depending on this spectrum:

\begin{description}
    \item[\textbf{{Sparse Page Size Spectra}
	    \index{page size spectrum!sparse} (Large $W$):}] On
	    architectures with a wide gap between base page sizes and
	    the first non-trivial superpage size, \pageclustering\ acts
	    as a bridge. However, this is treated as a trivial case: it
	    merely shaves an additive constant off the required order of
	    superpage allocations without changing underlying memory
	    management algorithms.

    The availability of contiguous physical frames exhibits an
	exponential (or even double-exponential) drop-off as the
	superpage order increases. Simultaneously, latency and internal
	fragmentation penalties scale aggressively beyond a threshold
	that is typically much smaller than the first non-trivial
	hardware superpage size. Consequently, reliable allocation is
	practically limited to the first few orders ($n \in \{1, 2\}$) —
	and occasionally order 3, despite the historical preferences of
	SPARC\index{SPARC} and Alpha\index{Alpha} partisans. Hence, a
	plausible $Z$ might be defined as:

\begin{align}
    Z &\approx 2^{-\Omega} g_1 \\
      &\approx K \cdot 2^{(W-\Omega)_+} \\
    B &\approx 2^{(W-\Omega)_+} \\
    \mathop{\mathrm{lg}}B = \beta &\approx (W-\Omega)_+
\end{align}

    Here, $\Omega$ can be tuned (where $\Omega = 2$ or
	$\Omega = 3$ are likely optimal) to balance the success rate of
	allocating superpages of the first nontrivial size ($g_1$)
	against the intensive latency mitigation and internal
	fragmentation management required to support such a $Z$. The
	larger the $Z$, the more aggressive these mitigation measures
	must be. Historical Linux porting experience — Dickins' 2003
	64\,GiB x86 boot and the subsequent ports to 22 architectures
	\footcite{dick01} — found approximately 256~KiB required
	minimal retrofit effort for interactive user workloads,
	whereas exceeding the Page Middle Directory size
	($\texttt{PAGE\_SIZE} > \texttt{PMD\_SIZE}$) introduced
	qualitative difficulties due to the truncation of the page table
	hierarchy.

    \item[\textbf{{Dense Spectra}\index{page size spectrum!dense} and
	    Allocation Guarantees:}] Where
		\pageclustering\ achieves a profound, qualitative
		breakthrough is for smaller superpage\index{superpaging}
		sizes. By maintaining physical allocations at the $Z$
		boundary, Telix\index{Telix} can \textit{guarantee that
		$Z$-sized physical allocations will never fail due to
		external fragmentation}.  The reason is structural: $Z$
		is the physical allocation unit, and the framekernel's
		LLFree allocator (\S\ref{sec:mem_coremapless}) is an
		\textit{extent} allocator, not a per-page buddy allocator.
		There is no smaller allocation that could split a $Z$-sized
		\textit{fragment} free extent.

		Allocation can still fail for reasons other than external
		fragmentation.  Global memory exhaustion is always possible,
		and an existing $Z$-sized extent might be pinned awaiting
		eviction by a page-replacement daemon.  But external
		fragmentation itself is eliminated as a failure mode.

		This obviates reserved-pool strategies for smaller
		superpage sizes (such as those used in {HP-UX}\index{HP-UX})
		and eliminates external fragmentation as a source of
		compaction pressure.  Compaction may still be needed for
		other reasons: NUMA placement, contiguity above $Z$, or
		pinned-frame availability.  The structural guarantee
		removes the most common trigger — unpredictable,
		runtime memory compaction that has occasionally afflicted
		Linux\index{Linux} — but it does not make compaction
		unnecessary.

\bigskip
\noindent
\textbf{Predicted Effect on the Superpage-Size Distribution
  (Hypothesis).}
Without \pageclustering, the expected frequency of translation sizes
in a typical workload follows a monotonically decreasing distribution:
most mappings use the smallest hardware page size ($K$), each successive
superpage size $g_n$ sees exponentially fewer mappings, and the tail
beyond $g_2$ is negligible.  This is the familiar power-law/exponential
contiguity drop-off of the buddy-allocator literature.

With \pageclustering, the allocation unit $Z$ acts as an attractor.
Because allocations of size $Z$ are guaranteed never to fail due to
external fragmentation, the memory manager preferentially (and
reliably) serves requests at that boundary.  The expected distribution
shifts from a monotone decreasing exponential to a \textbf{unimodal,
right-skewed bell}:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A short left tail from $K$ up to $Z$, representing residual
        smaller mappings (incremental COW faults, tail-packing fragments);
  \item A peak at $Z$, where the bulk of allocations concentrate;
  \item A right tail above $Z$ that resumes the exponential drop-off
        of the unclustered distribution, reflecting truly huge mappings
        (database buffer pools, HPC arrays) that can exploit
        hardware superpages when contiguity permits.
\end{itemize}

The dashed line often drawn through the right tail in sketches of this
distribution is the \emph{unclustered} exponential — the curve that
would obtain without the $Z$-attractor, decaying from $K$ downward.
Page clustering effectively carves a plateau around $Z$ out of what
would otherwise be a far steeper drop.

This shift has practical consequences for TLB-management policy:
because the modal allocation size is $Z$, the TLB refill path can be
optimized for $Z$-sized translations, with fast-paths for smaller
($K$-sized) fault fills and slow-paths for the uncommon large superpages.

More fundamentally, \emph{if} the distributional shift materialises
as hoped, it raises the \textbf{expected translation size} and
therefore the \textbf{TLB reach}.

\medskip\noindent
\textbf{The TLB-architecture precondition.}
This argument presupposes a TLB whose reach can actually vary with
the translation-size distribution — i.e., a \emph{unified} or
size-tagged TLB in which each entry can cache any translation size.
Intel's size-segregated TLB hierarchy (separate arrays for 4~KiB
and 2~MiB entries) defeats this: the 4~KiB array cannot cache a
64~KiB translation, so herding the distribution toward $Z$ does not
expand reach in that array.  The custom MMU's residue-partitioned
TLB (\S\ref{sec:custom_mmu}) is explicitly designed to avoid this
trap: four partitions keyed by $\lg g \bmod 4$ each span the full
size range, so every partition's reach grows with the mean
translation size.  On architectures with segregated TLBs, the
benefit is limited to whatever array natively holds the $Z$-sized
translations.

\medskip\noindent
\textbf{The hoped-for distributional shift.}
Without \pageclustering, the translation-size distribution is
approximately geometric (monotonically decreasing): the overwhelming
majority of mappings use the smallest page size $K$, each successive
superpage size sees exponentially fewer mappings, and the tail beyond
$g_2$ is negligible.  The TLB reach — $\text{TLB entries} \times
\mathbb{E}[\text{translation size}]$ — is correspondingly small, and
TLB misses are dominated by capacity evictions of $K$-sized entries.

The hope is that \pageclustering, by guaranteeing that $Z$-sized
allocations never fail due to external fragmentation, shifts this
distribution from a geometric decay to something resembling a
unimodal, right-skewed bell (informally, a negative-binomial-shaped
distribution with mode near $Z$).  The mass that would have been
concentrated at $K$ is redistributed toward $Z$, and the mean
translation size rises from $\approx K$ toward $Z$.  \emph{If} this
shift occurs and the TLB is unified (as above), then a modest rise in
the mean — say, from 4~KiB to 64~KiB — would yield a $16\times$
expansion of TLB coverage with no change to the hardware TLB, and the
TLB miss rate would drop accordingly.

This is a hoped-for outcome, not a theorem.  The structural guarantee
(no external fragmentation at $Z$) is proved; the distributional
consequence (the shift from geometric to unimodal) is a prediction
requiring empirical validation.  The interaction with replacement
policy under realistic workloads is an additional unknown: within a
$Z$-extent, individual fragments may be in mixed states (some clean,
some dirty, some pinned), and the time to drain a mixed extent
before returning it to the free pool could introduce latency
variability in the replacement path.  Telix's design guarantees that
$Z$-extents are never fragmented into sub-$Z$ pieces, but the
replacement daemon must still handle the case where only some
fragments of a $Z$-extent are immediately reclaimable.

Very large superpages ($\gg Z$) are still needed for extreme-footprint
workloads (database buffer pools, HPC arrays), but these are served by
the long right tail of the distribution and addressed through orthogonal
mechanisms (hardware superpage promotion, contiguous allocation hints).
\pageclustering's hoped-for contribution is to address the \emph{left}
side of the distribution — the mass of small mappings that would
otherwise dominate TLB pressure — and to pull it toward $Z$.

\medskip
\noindent
\textbf{Prior-work validation and the combination burden.}
The individual mechanisms that compose into the page-clustering
architecture carry their own validation from prior work: LLFree's
lock-free latency characteristics \footcite{litz2021llfree},
superpageblocks' production measurements at Meta
\footcite{riel26superpageblocks}, Dickins' historic x86 and
multi-architecture page-clustering ports
\footcite{dick01}, and io\_uring's throughput benchmarks
\footcite{axboe19iouring}.  The \emph{combination} of these mechanisms
into a single system is an additional evaluation burden belonging to
the Telix project itself.

    \item[\textbf{Small Minimum Mapping Granularities ($K$):}] There are
	    few lower-bound constraints on $K$. In principle, user PTEs
		need not even be smaller than the mapped memory; page
		table space conservation techniques can enable at least
		one user process to run effectively. Page clustering
		itself does not demand space for sub-$Z$ mappings apart
		from the PTEs, and certainly not on any physical
		accounting basis, rendering VAX-like\index{VAX}
		scenarios eminently feasible without conflicting with
		advanced architectural features. The 1~KiB
		{MIPS\index{MIPS} \texttt{PageGrain} extension}
		\index{PageGrain@\texttt{PageGrain} extension (MIPS)}
		serves as the most plausible hardware support vehicle
		for demonstrating the extreme range of supportable
		mapping granularities.
\end{description}

\section{Pervasive Extent-Based Accounting and Coremaplessness}
\label{sec:mem_coremapless}

Sublinear physical memory management metadata is achieved in Telix
\index{Telix} not through \pageclustering, but
through \textit{Pervasive Extent-Based Internal Accounting} and true
\textit{Coremaplessness}.

Traditional operating systems reserve a contiguous array of physical
{page descriptors}\index{page descriptor} at boot time to track frame
metadata. This imposes a severe penalty in the context of Wulf and
McKee's ``Memory Wall''\footcite{wulf95memorywall}. In modern execution
environments, CPU speeds vastly outpace memory access times, making
cache sizes the true bottleneck. The global metadata arrays of legacy
kernels consume critical cache space, putting kernel bookkeeping in
direct competition with user workloads for L2 and L3 cache residency.
Because TLBs\index{TLB} typically feature kernel-specific optimizations
or support superpages\index{superpaging} large enough to direct-map
physical memory with minimal entries, the ``Memory Wall'' is primarily a
cache-pressure phenomenon rather than a TLB\index{TLB} bottleneck.

Telix\index{Telix} intends to achieve a coremapless architecture by
utilizing physical addresses—specifically {Page Frame Numbers (PFNs)}
\index{page frame number} quantized by $Z$—as the direct, raw handles
for allocation and deallocation operations.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dynamic Subsystem Metadata:} Rather than preallocating
	    metadata globally for every frame, any required metadata is
		allocated dynamically strictly within the specific
		subsystems (e.g., the page cache) that need it.
    \item \textbf{Extent-Based Indexing:} When frames are tracked within
	    these subsystem index structures, they are not indexed as
		singular pages. Pervasive extent-based accounting
		dictates that groups of them are tracked as power-of-two
		sized, power-of-two aligned memory ranges.
\end{itemize}

To manage the raw physical frames underneath this accounting layer,
Telix\index{Telix} employs a lock-free adaptation of the \textit{LLFree}
\index{LLFree@\texttt{LLFree}} allocator\footcite{litz2021llfree}. The
choice of \texttt{LLFree} is deliberate, yet incidental to
coremaplessness itself: \texttt{LLFree} is utilized primarily because it
avoids the cache misses and heavy lock contention associated with
traditional buddy allocators (such as Linux's, which manipulates linked
lists embedded inside the global page descriptor array). By relying on
in-band metadata within free frames, \texttt{LLFree} further preserves
the coremapless cache profile.

\medskip
\noindent
\textbf{Independent validation: the Linux superpageblocks work.}
While Telix approaches contiguity at the allocator's design level,
the Linux memory-management community has independently converged on
the same underlying problem from the opposite direction.  Van Riel's
recent ``superpageblocks'' series
\footcite{riel26superpageblocks} divides the buddy allocator into
PUD-sized (1\,GiB) regions and applies placement policy to steer
unmovable and reclaimable allocations into already-tainted blocks,
keeping clean blocks available for 1\,GiB and 2\,MiB huge-page
allocations.  Migration evacuates mixed blocks below a free threshold,
and movable allocations are directed to the fullest clean blocks to
maximise contiguity headroom elsewhere.

The superpageblocks concept is, in effect, the buddy-allocator
equivalent of the morsel: a large, contiguously-managed allocation
region whose internal fragmentation state is tracked and whose
external contiguity is preserved by policy.  Where Telix's morsels
make this the \emph{primary} accounting unit (eschewing per-4KiB-page
metadata entirely), van Riel's series retrofits the same structure
atop the existing per-page \texttt{mem\_map[]}.  Both approaches
recognise that the fundamental operational granularity of a modern
memory manager should be a multi-page extent, not a single hardware
page.  That the problem is urgent enough for Meta to fund work at this
scale — 40 patches across the core page allocator, compaction, and
migration — is the strongest independent validation that the morsel
approach addresses a real, production-critical bottleneck.

\section{Virtual Metadata, Hardware Walkers, and Inverted Page Tables}
\label{sec:mem_virtual_metadata}

Addressing virtual memory metadata overhead presents a distinct set of
trade-offs, dictated heavily by CPU architecture design choices.

\subsection{Mitigating Hardware Page-Table Walkers}

A major challenge in modern CPU design is the presence of mandatory
hardware page-table walkers. On architectures that enforce multi-level
hierarchical page tables (e.g., x86-64, ARM64, RISC-V), hardware walkers
render pure Inverted Page Tables (IPT) unusable for direct hardware MMU
fills.

To mitigate virtual metadata bloat under mandatory hardware walkers,
Telix\index{Telix} proposes \textit{Extent-Driven Page Table Sharing}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Canonical address space mappings are maintained independently
	    of the hardware page tables in highly compressed software
		structures, combining \textit{Adaptive Radix Trees
		(ART)} (for dense extents) and \textit{B+ Trees} (for
		sparse ranges).
    \item While these software structures were initially conceived for
	    pure software-refill architectures, they provide a powerful
		coordination layer for mitigating hardware walkers. By
		using the ART/B+ trees to canonicalize large memory
		ranges, the OS can easily identify massive contiguous
		shared regions.
    \item Telix leverages this software extent knowledge to safely link
	    or clone the actual underlying hardware page table sub-trees
		across multiple processes. This allows thousands of
		processes to share a shared library or IPC ring while
		sharing the physical PTE structures, drastically
		reducing virtual memory footprints.
    \item To avoid cache-line contention and lock bottlenecks on
	    multi-core systems, shared page table nodes will employ
		localized replication mechanisms.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Inverted Page Tables and Software-Refill Architectures}

On CPU architectures featuring software-managed TLB refills (e.g., MIPS,
software-refill RISC-V profiles) or native hardware IPT support (e.g.,
64-bit SPARC, IA-64 Virtual Hash Page Tables [VHPT], and IBM POWER
Hashed Page Tables [HPT] and Segment Lookaside Buffers [SLB]), Telix
will actively utilize Inverted Page Tables.

In these environments, the IPT contains one entry per physical frame,
indexed via a hash of the Virtual Address and Address Space ID (ASID).
Telix will explore whether hardware-unassisted IPTs in pure
software-refill architectures provide a net performance benefit over
direct ART/B+ extent tree walks, treating software-refill IPT
optimizations as a primary experimental benchmark for the project.

\subsection{Custom Inverted-Page-Table MMU (satp modes 14/15)}
\label{sec:custom_mmu}

As a research prototype demonstrating the full range of the
\pageclustering\ thesis, Telix specifies a forward-looking RISC-V
MMU extension that replaces the hardware radix-tree walker with an
\emph{inverted (hashed) page table}.

\begin{description}
  \item[\textbf{Two additional \texttt{satp} modes.}] Mode 14 selects
    the bare inverted page table (hash-based, no hardware walker);
    mode 15 adds a POWER9-style Segment Lookaside Buffer (SLB) as a
    gating cache.  The existing Sv39/Sv48/Sv57 modes are unaffected.
    Because there is no radix tree to walk, the full 64-bit virtual
    address space is used rather than truncating at 48 or 57 bits.

  \item[\textbf{42 distinct translation sizes.}] The 56 bits above
    the 256~B offset are split into a 14-bit segment ID (16,384
    segments) and a 42-bit VPN within the segment.  With a base page
    $K = 256$~B (half the VAX-11's 512~B) and a jump increment
    $W = 1$, the extension represents $g_n = K \cdot 2^{n}$ for
    $n \in [0, 41]$, from 256~B to 512~TiB — 42 translation sizes in
    all, or 41 superpage sizes strictly above the base page.  On a
    conventional radix tree a 256~B page would be catastrophic (a
    48-bit VA yields $2^{40}$ pages and a 16-level tree).  An
    inverted table is sized to the number of \emph{mappings}, not
    pages, so larger translations \emph{shrink} the table.
    \pageclustering\ supplies the fine-granularity COW/sharing
    behaviour at the bottom while superpages supply TLB reach at the
    top.

  \item[\textbf{Partner-hashed table.}] The table uses partner
    hashing (Bender--Kuszmaul--Zhou) to run at load factor 1 with
    $O(1)$ expected queries and high-probability $O(1)$
    insert/delete, with the permutation of entries encoding per-entry
    metadata rather than storing it explicitly.  The hash is
    superpage-aware: all virtual addresses within one superpage hash
    to the same slot.

  \item[\textbf{Residue-partitioned TLB.}] Four TLB partitions are
    keyed by $\lg g \bmod 4$, so each partition spans the
    \emph{entire} size range and the TLB remains expansible — unlike
    Intel's segregated TLB hierarchy, which can never cache a
    superpage in its base-page arrays.

  \item[\textbf{SLB (mode 15).}] A 256-entry segment cache maps a
    1~PiB segment — the 50-bit segment boundary implied by the
    14-bit segment ID — to its segment-table pointer, narrowing the
    inverted-table probe on hit.  The segment exceeds the largest
    superpage ($2^{49}$~B), so no single superpage crosses a segment
    boundary.
\end{description}

\medskip
\noindent
\textbf{No existing operating system could operate this MMU.}
Every general-purpose operating system in wide use assumes a radix-tree
page table and a small, fixed page-size spectrum (typically a 4~KiB
base page plus one or two huge-page sizes, e.g. 2~MiB/1~GiB on x86).
Their allocators, page-table walkers, TLB shootdown paths, and
reverse-mapping structures are all specialised to that assumption, and
the 256~B base page defeats them from two directions at once.

First, almost every kernel allocates a per-page descriptor array sized
to the number of hardware pages.  At 256~B the overhead is $16\times$
that at 4~KiB for the same physical RAM, so the metadata would rival or
exceed the memory it tracks.  Second, the few kernels that might
dress the inverted table up as a legacy radix-tree MMU to reuse
existing walker and shootdown logic hit the same wall: a 256~B base
page over a 64-bit VA would require a radix tree of roughly sixteen
levels, whose page-table overhead dwarfs the mapped memory.  The dense
42-size spectrum cannot be flattened onto a fixed multi-level walk
without abandoning both the fine granularity and the inverted table's
size-proportional-to-mappings property.

This MMU is therefore drivable only by a kernel whose memory
management is built around extents and an inverted structure from the
start — which is precisely what Telix provides, through its morsel
allocator, extent-based accounting, and software-managed refill with a
verified handler.  That is the strongest expression of the claim that
page clustering is not an incremental change to existing memory
management but a different point in the design space.

The extension is modelled in Sail and mechanically generated to Rocq,
with machine-checked proofs of hash determinism, superpage-consistency,
partition well-definedness, and TLB-coverage agreement in the Tessera
hardware model, and a QEMU simulation branch exercising modes 14 and 15.
It is a research prototype: hardware implementation would require
RISC-V ratification of the new \texttt{satp} encodings.

\section{Low-Latency Optimization: Incremental Faulting and Tail Packing}
\label{sec:mem_latency_opt}

To minimize I/O and fault latency, the proposed memory architecture
incorporates two key structural mechanisms:

\begin{description}
    \item[\textbf{Incremental Anonymous Memory Faulting:}] For
	    Copy-On-Write (COW) and Zero-Fill-On-Demand (ZFOD) anonymous
		memory, Telix will implement incremental fault handling.
		Rather than allocating and zeroing full allocation
		blocks ($Z$) on initial access, the framekernel's LLFree
		allocator provisions a $Z$-sized physical frame but
		faults sub-fragments ($K$) one at a time as they are
		accessed, reducing initial fault stalls and optimising
		bitblitting latency for interactive applications.

    \item[\textbf{In-Memory Tail Packing:}] Small files and trailing
	    file fragments in the page cache frequently waste memory due
		to internal alignment padding. Telix plans to support
		in-memory tail packing, packing sub-page file tails into
		shared physical frames to maximize cache efficiency and
		reduce storage bus pressure.
\end{description}

\section{Hardware Capability Bounds: CHERI and Morello}
\label{sec:mem_cheri}

The extent-based accounting used throughout the VM subsystem aligns
naturally with hardware-enforced capability architectures, specifically
\textit{CHERI} and ARM Morello.

When compiled for capability hardware, the (base, length) bounds of
a capability morsel token are materialised as the bounds field of a
128-bit hardware fat pointer (CHERI capability).  This transfers
spatial memory safety enforcement from software checks to the CPU
hardware pipeline, providing unforgeable, hardware-bounded memory
protection for shared-memory I/O rings and Framekernel capability
domains.

\section{Framekernels, Multikernels, and Address Spaces}
\label{sec:mem_address_spaces}

\Asterinas's \framekernel
\footcite{peng24} methodology shares a single kernel address space
across the lowest-level domains it covers. Conversely, \Barrelfish's
\multikernel\footcite{baum09} utilizes strictly separate kernel address
spaces for the domains it manages.

Telix leverages the \multikernel\ approach to establish
``soft partitions'' across the nodes of a Single System Image (SSI)
\index{SSI (Single System Image)} cluster, geographically bounding
domains within which scalability\index{scalability} remains physically
and computationally feasible.  Partitions are sized at the NUMA-node
granularity (not per-core, as in the original Barrelfish model) to
align protection boundaries with hardware affinity.

Within these soft partitions, Telix selectively adapts the
\framekernel\ technique: thin, performance-critical server layers
share the kernel address space under Rust-based safety enforcement,
while remaining servers run in their own address spaces as conventional
microkernel processes.  This hybrid construction \emph{reduces} the
frequency of {address space} context switches on latency-critical
paths, without eliminating them entirely.  The boundary between
in-framekernel and out-of-framekernel servers is experimental and
may be adjusted as the design matures (see
\S\ref{ch:control-plane}).
